WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.758
Domain Name System, DNS, is the service of choice for resolving hostnames to IP addresses. Take a look at this DNS setup on Windows Server.

00:10.758 --> 00:20.064
Here, we see a couple of hostnames on the network and their IP addresses. In a Windows environment, DNS is set up when Active Directory is set up.

00:20.064 --> 00:24.982
Or, DNS can be installed as a role on a server that is not a domain controller.

00:24.982 --> 00:34.323
This DNS server you see is an example of an internal DNS server as it is only resolving hostnames on this internal network.

00:34.323 --> 00:44.695
Many DNS servers are external DNS servers, which resolve Internet names to addresses. Take, for example, a simple ping command.

00:44.695 --> 01:02.066
On this device, I'm going to ping www.google.com. Somewhere, a DNS server had to resolve this hostname to this IP address.

01:02.066 --> 01:09.141
There are actually 13 root DNS servers worldwide that have these massive tables of hostnames and IP addresses.

01:09.141 --> 01:18.352
Having DNS sure beats the alternative, which is that you would have to memorize the IP address of every website and host you visit. That would not be fun.

01:18.352 --> 01:37.136
Speaking of DNS servers, to find out what DNS server you are running, run the ipconfig /all command. It will tell you in order which DNS servers are being applied to your device.

01:37.136 --> 01:44.923
Here, I have a couple of external DNS servers followed by three internal DNS servers.

01:44.923 --> 01:54.500
This means that we should have no problem resolving both internal and external hostnames to IP addresses.

